// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-004
//
// PROBE 09 — a `comptime` pattern walker. **EXPECTED REFUSED**, and this probe
// exists to record exactly HOW.
//
// ASKS: a `comptime func:` that indexes a pattern string byte by byte and
// returns the position of the first `(`.
//
// MATTERS BECAUSE it is **O-N1**'s evidence, and O-N1 is the most valuable
// request on this library's list. A compile-time-validated pattern literal — a
// malformed pattern becoming a **compile error**, at the line that wrote it,
// with no run-time cost and no error path — is a property no regex library in
// any language offers. `#regex("(a|b")` would be a diagnostic instead of a
// `PatternError` nobody checks.
//
// PREDICTED AT PLANNING from the compiler's 1.5.0 source: `fold_expr` in
// `src/frontend/resolve_type.npk` has no arm for an index expression, and
// `fold_string_builtin` handles exactly four names, none of which reads a byte.
//
// THE DIAGNOSTIC, verbatim, from compiler commit 950bb1d:
//
//   NITPICK-TYPE-004 …:77:16: `comptime` requires an expression that folds at
//   compile time, and this one does not
//
// It is clean about the FACT and silent about the CAUSE — it does not say which
// sub-expression refused to fold, which is the one thing a reader needs. That
// is worth saying in the request: the fix is cheap and the diagnostic is not.
//
// ==========================================================================
// AND THE WALL IS ONE STEP EARLIER THAN THE PLAN PREDICTED
// ==========================================================================
//
// The prediction was "no arm for an INDEX expression". Measured by isolation —
// nine `comptime func:` bodies, each adding one construct, every one compiled
// and its exit code recorded (the table is in `tests/probe/TRANSCRIPT.txt`):
//
//     plain constant                        FOLDS
//     mutable local + arithmetic            FOLDS
//     counted `while` loop                  FOLDS
//     string_byte_length                    FOLDS
//     string_is_empty                       FOLDS
//     string_equals                         FOLDS
//     string_concat                         FOLDS
//     string_bytes, then `.len`             NITPICK-TYPE-004
//     string_bytes, then an INDEX           NITPICK-TYPE-004
//
// **`string_bytes` is the wall, and `.len` alone is already past it.** The
// index never gets a chance, because the view is never produced. `string_bytes`
// is not one of the four names `fold_string_builtin` handles — the four are
// exactly the four that fold above — so O-N1's ask is more precisely stated as
// *"fold `string_bytes`, and index the slice it yields"*, which is two arms
// rather than one.
//
// THE GOOD NEWS FOR THE REQUEST, and it is what makes it arguable: **loops,
// mutable locals and every string operation this library's validator would
// need to REPORT with already fold.** A pattern's length, an equality against a
// known prefix and a concatenated message are all available at compile time
// today. What is missing is exclusively the ability to look at a byte.
mod:probe09_comptime_walker;

// The walker. Nothing here is exotic: a loop, an index, a compare. Every one of
// those works at RUN time — this same function without `comptime` is ordinary
// code, which is what makes the gap a gap rather than a design.
comptime func:first_paren = int64(string:s) {
    uint8[]:b = string_bytes(s);
    int64:i = 0i64;
    while (i < b.len) {
        if (b[i] == 40u8) { pass i; }        // '('
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    pass -1i64;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    // The forcing form: `comptime(…)` in a value position must fold at check
    // time. This is the line that cannot be folded.
    int64:at = comptime(first_paren("ab(cd"));
    if (at != 2i64) { exit 10i32; }
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
